      "writtenOn": "2023-08-11T08:49:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Question: What is the reason we need this flag?\n  Is it because there is no common way for a caller to specify a NetworkRequest with the below use cases? By using this flag, all cases can be achieved:\n\n1. The caller only wants to see local networks.\n  -\u003e NetworkCallback(FLAG_INCLUDE_LOCAL_NETWORKS) + NetworkRequest(NET_CAPABILITY_LOCAL_NETWORK)\n2. The caller only wants to see non-local networks.\n  -\u003e NetworkCallback() + NetworkRequest()\n3. The caller wants to see all networks.\n  -\u003e NetworkCallback(FLAG_INCLUDE_LOCAL_NETWORKS) + NetworkRequest()\n\nIf so, it seems to me the FLAG_INCLUDE_LOCAL_NETWORKS flag is a ad-hoc solution just for local networks, but it sounds to me it is not flexible enough to handle the other cases. For example, if we want to support use similar cases of suspended, metered, roaming, congested networks, we would need to create a new flag for each type of cap, right?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 8995,
        "startChar": 46,
        "endLine": 8995,
        "endChar": 73
      },
      "revId": "e8f08b9d2e5f9e081619538aecadf4d0c228ff88",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "06e101e8_3537e667",
        "filename": "service/src/com/android/server/ConnectivityService.java",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 8995,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-14T11:44:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Gave an explanation 2 patches ago, but in a nutshell this is for backward compatibility. Existing apps don\u0027t expect getting callbacks about downstream networks, and it\u0027s really hard to predict how they\u0027d handle this. Imagine an app listening for wifi. Now they get a callback about tethering downstream. It transport is wifi. What\u0027s it going to do ? Maybe it will notice there is no INTERNET and it won\u0027t try to use it. But unfortunately most apps are not very well written, and this will likely result in a lot of confusion.\n\nTo avoid issues, we therefore require opt-in. That\u0027s what this flag is for.\n\nYou are right that this doesn\u0027t scale, but this case doesn\u0027t happen every other day. For example, the last 2 caps we introduced, PRIORITIZE_{LATENCY,BANDWIDTH} do not warrant such a flag – they are additional things a network can do, not completely new types of networks that it might be bad sending to existing apps. The same is true of PARTIAL_CONNECTIVITY, TEMP_NOT_METERED, NOT_VCN_MANAGED, ENTERPRISE etc.\nYou could argue that some of the caps could have warranted this in the past, like HEAD_UNIT, but we didn\u0027t do it. But if we were to introduce NOT_METERED today, we\u0027d certainly do this : imagine Android only ever tells you about unmetered networks and suddenly it starts telling you about metered networks – we\u0027d want opt-in.",
      "parentUuid": "b6854512_a054e5eb",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 8995,
        "startChar": 46,
        "endLine": 8995,
        "endChar": 73
      },
      "revId": "e8f08b9d2e5f9e081619538aecadf4d0c228ff88",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bfeb58c7_26d14dd8",
